            "passage": "For nine-year-old Hana, the old playground at the end of her block was the best place in the world. It had a rusty slide that squeaked, a single roundabout and two swings. Nothing about it was new or exciting. But it was where Hana learned to pump the swings on her own, where she made her best friend Siti on a rainy Wednesday afternoon, and where she always went when she felt sad or happy or simply restless.\n\nOne afternoon, Hana came home from school to find a notice pasted on the gate. The playground would be demolished the following week to make way for a new multi-purpose fitness area.\n\nHana felt a sudden tightness in her chest. She ran to the playground, sat on her favourite swing and pushed off slowly. The chain creaked. The sky was turning orange.\n\n'Are you upset?' asked a voice. It was Siti, sitting on the other swing.\n\n'It's just a playground,' Hana said. But her voice was quiet.\n\n'I know,' said Siti. 'But it was our playground.'\n\nThe two of them swung in silence as the sun went down. When the streetlights came on, they finally walked home. Hana did not say anything, but she felt a little lighter.\n\nThe new fitness area opened three months later. It had bright equipment and a smooth path for jogging. Hana used it sometimes. But she never forgot the rusty swing that squeaked.",
