            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": "EVERYONE / NOBODY / SOMEONE / EACH = Singular! Always use verb+S. Everyone CHOOSES = add -S. 'Everyone choose' is WRONG. Singular indefinite pronouns always take singular present-tense verbs.",
            "hints": [
              "The subject is EVERYONE, not the members of the club. Words like everyone and nobody act like ONE person. Ask: in the present tense, does a singular subject take the plain verb, or the verb ending in -s?"
            ]
