            "passage": "Twelve-year-old Farah found a black notebook on the seat of Bus 67 on a Wednesday afternoon. She nearly left it where it was — it was not hers, and she was already running late. But she picked it up.\n\nThe notebook had no name on the cover. Inside, she found page after page of pencil drawings — birds, trees, faces, and at the back, a half-finished sketch of a street that looked familiar. She turned to the first page. Underneath the first drawing was a small inscription: 'For whoever finds this — please keep it safe.'\n\nFarah brought the notebook home and placed it on her desk. She looked at the drawings every evening that week. She thought about the person who made them — someone careful, someone patient, someone who looked at the world closely.\n\nShe decided she had to return it. She went back to the bus stop and spoke to the driver of Bus 67. He did not recognise the notebook but told her to check with the transport company's lost property office.\n\nAt the lost property office, a young man was already waiting. He looked up when Farah walked in, saw the notebook in her hands and stood up quickly.\n\n'That's mine,' he said.\n\nFarah handed it over. The young man held the notebook for a moment and exhaled slowly. 'I looked for this for two weeks,' he said. 'Thank you.'\n\nOn the bus home, Farah felt lighter than she had in a long time.",
