            "passage": "The sign at the entrance to the freshwater zone read: 'Seven percent of all known animal species live in freshwater. Yet freshwater makes up less than one percent of the Earth's habitable surface.'\n\nTwelve-year-old Theo read the sign twice. He had always thought of rivers as ordinary. He grew up next to the Kallang River and never thought much about what lived beneath its surface.\n\nInside the exhibit, he pressed his face against the glass of the largest tank. A fat, slow-moving arapaima drifted past — one of the largest freshwater fish in the world, over two metres long. Beside it, a school of silver dollar fish caught the light and flashed like coins.\n\nA keeper in a green vest appeared. 'Do you know how they breathe?' she asked Theo, nodding at the arapaima.\n\nTheo shook his head.\n\n'They can breathe air,' she said. 'Their swim bladder works like a lung. During dry season when rivers shrink and oxygen levels drop, they swim to the surface and gulp air.'\n\nTheo stared. 'So they survive conditions that would kill most fish?'\n\n'Exactly,' said the keeper. 'They've been doing it for two hundred million years.'\n\nTheo looked at the arapaima again. It was not beautiful in the way that coral reef fish were beautiful. But there was something about it — something ancient and unstoppable — that he found more impressive.",
