            "passage": "Most people know that bees make honey. But the engineering inside a beehive is just as remarkable as the honey itself.\n\nBees build their hives from wax, which they produce inside their own bodies. A single worker bee produces only about one gram of wax in its entire lifetime. Yet a large hive may contain over one hundred thousand individual wax cells, all built to exactly the same six-sided shape.\n\nThe six-sided shape, called a hexagon, is not chosen by accident. It is the most efficient shape that exists: it uses the least amount of wax to create the most amount of storage space. Engineers and architects have studied the hexagon for centuries because of this property.\n\nInside each cell, bees store honey, pollen or eggs. The temperature inside the hive is kept at exactly thirty-five degrees Celsius at all times — warm enough for eggs to develop, cool enough to preserve the honey.\n\nWhen a hive becomes too crowded, the queen bee leaves with half the colony to build a new home. The remaining bees raise a new queen. In this way, the colony survives and grows, generation after generation.",
